/* ── wp-admin ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The admin pages themselves. main.css goes in through add_editor_style(),
   which puts it inside the editable area, so it cannot reach the furniture
   around it. This is enqueued on the admin page instead.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* The classic editor shows fields, not content.
   Every section of this site is built from its own field group and drawn by
   its component; the post's own content is not used and is empty on the
   pages that matter. An editor opening one is there for the fields, so the
   content box is taken out of their way.

   Hidden, not removed from the form — the field keeps posting what it was
   given, so a save carries the content back unchanged and autosave keeps
   working on it. #postdivrich is the classic editor's own wrapper and exists
   on no other screen. ACF's own wysiwyg fields are separate elements and are
   left alone: those are what the classic view is for. */
#postdivrich {
    display: none;
}

/* The publish box: its links read as the buttons they are, and the publish
   action leaves the absolute corner core parks it in. */
#major-publishing-actions {
    a {
        display: inline-block;
        padding: 10px 16px;
        line-height: 1.5;
    }

    #publishing-action {
        position: relative !important;
        right: 0 !important;
        bottom: 0 !important;
    }
}
